The computed boot sweep, in full
   The sweep played the **current synodic cycle** — from the last new moon (a dark disk) to *now*, at most 29.53 days — in ``UI_MOON_ANIM_FRAMES`` frames (1344 since :commit:`57aa0cae`, when the per-frame illumination jump at the old 336-frame density read as a visible stutter). The span came from ``new_moon_epoch``, a two-pass backward scan of ``moon_geometry``'s illumination (a 6 h coarse grid over one synodic month, then a 20-min fine pass around the coarse minimum, the 1e-6 slack keeping a tied coarse step from drifting into a neighbouring cycle). A core-1 bake task produced the frames **off screen** and published them one at a time; the tick displayed each as one atomic whole frame (the whole-panel ``lv_refr_now``, measured 304 ms S3 / 207 ms P4), and the **backpressure** rule — the bake did not start frame *f*+1 until the tick had displayed frame *f* — kept a torn frame off screen. Because the display was faster than the bake (~1.3 s at native resolution), the bake paced the sweep: a full-res boot ran ~36.5 min (S3, 1344 frames) / ~40 min (P4), intermediate frames baked at native ``MOON_PX`` (400 / 650) since :commit:`7c3a39d1`, so the montage was pixel-identical to the static bake. The per-frame cost carried over unchanged to the deferred re-bake and the hourly producer, which bake at the same native resolution and hit the same 864-albedo PSRAM load-latency floor.

   The sweep's TF card cache drove a prefetch task (``moon_tfprefetch``, core 0), a replay pacing (``UI_MOON_TFCACHE_FPS``) and a PSRAM slot floor (``UI_MOON_TFCACHE_PREFETCH_MIN_SLOTS``); all three retired with the sweep, and the config sweep deleted the keys. Two facts about the surviving module shaped it: the folder is keyed on the commitizen-set version (the ``version`` line in ``.cz.toml``, which ``cz bump`` writes; the ``git describe`` short hash keys it only until the first bump), so **the card re-keys on a deliberate version bump** (the first bump 0.1.0 → 0.2.0, :commit:`97d1ebd7`) and the stale sibling folders are pruned then — plain commits leave the card alone (the old per-commit ``git describe`` key did start the cache from zero, ``-dirty`` suffix and all, until :commit:`56ce4dea`); and the decode out-buffer is allocated **lazily** on the first hit rather than at init (:commit:`0dd75745`) — holding the ``3·S²`` buffer eagerly starved the 8 MB S3's slot floor and silently killed the card path every boot, so the cache could never warm.

The dusk, in full
   The dusk was the light-sleep entry. It rotated the camera about the Moon's **pole** (body-frame +z) at the current phase, 1°/frame along the shortest arc to the fully unilluminated side (14–95 s, display-paced at ~0.2 s/frame), then armed the deep-sleep countdown; sleep depth followed the animation — the countdown fired only once the dark side was on screen. It was cheap because of the **constant-projection property** (:doc:`/science/darkside`, :doc:`/implementation/core-c`): a pixel's color depends on the body-frame surface direction and the epoch's fixed Sun only — the view basis selects *which* direction is visible but never enters the *color* — so the full body-frame shade map was **built once** for the epoch (``moon_shade_map_build``, ~1.6 s S3 / ~282 ms P4) and every frame **re-projected** it through the folded camera basis (a ``moon_rotate``-class gather, ~50 ms, not a re-bake). A fixed surface point kept exactly its map cell's color on every frame, so the terminator could not flicker. The endpoint is where :math:`\mathbf{E}\cdot\mathbf{S}` is minimized (the disk fully night-side; the shortest-arc minimum :math:`\phi^* = \mathrm{atan2}(-B,\,-A)`, host-verified against a full-circle grid), and with :math:`\mathbf{E}` the orbit-folded view direction (the tilt peeks the view, so the endpoint was computed from the folded, not the plain, :math:`\mathbf{E}`). The **final frame** was one full-``MOON_PX`` bake at ``phi*``; its thin sun-lit rim was handled by the ``dk_sup`` backlit-sliver parameter (the ``MOON_DK_MARGIN`` nearest-neighbour erosion killing the "white ray"). The shade-map and pole-rotate entry points stay in the portable core, exercised by the shared host tools; every device bake passes ``dk_sup`` 0, and :doc:`/science/darkside` documents the model.

The free spin, in full
   The spin was the ball-in-hand burn-in rotation: a once-baked frame re-projected by the Q15.15 gather at 8 fps and written directly to the panel, bypassing LVGL for that frame (the product verdict: *"It does more things worse than good."*). Its keep-mask was a full-screen ``lv_snapshot`` overlay that kept the clock legible while the Moon rotated beneath the direct writes. It displayed 8 fps off a once-baked frame, which was also the frame rate the rotation and gray→RGB565 functions were latency-optimized for (:doc:`/implementation/rotate-lut`). The spin and its keep-mask went in :commit:`79937e37`.
